ref('taubes1982nonminimal1','Taubes, Clifford Henry','The existence of a non-minimal solution to the {SU(2) Yang--Mills--Higgs} equations on {$\\mathbb R^3$}. Part I',1982,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01206014',journal='Communications in Mathematical Physics',volume=86,pages='257--298',doi='10.1007/BF01206014',annote='Publisher abstract and metadata opened. Published June 1982; received 5 May 1982. Part I establishes the variational sufficient condition; full original proof was inaccessible.')
ref('taubes1982nonminimal2','Taubes, Clifford Henry','The existence of a non-minimal solution to the {SU(2) Yang--Mills--Higgs} equations on {$\\mathbb R^3$}: Part II',1982,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01212170',journal='Communications in Mathematical Physics',volume=86,pages='299--320',doi='10.1007/BF01212170',annote='Publisher abstract and metadata opened. Published September 1982; received 5 May 1982. Abstract states finite-action non-Bogomolny solution and noncontractible-loop method. Charge-sector details of the full proof were not read.')
ref('taubes1985minmax','Taubes, Clifford Henry','Min-max theory for the {Yang--Mills--Higgs} equations',1985,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01221215',journal='Communications in Mathematical Physics',volume=97,pages='473--540',doi='10.1007/BF01221215',annote='Publisher abstract and metadata opened; each monopole sector, infinitely many solutions, unbounded energy. Received 14 May 1983, revised 25 June 1984. Must be read with the 1989 erratum; full proof not read here.')
ref('taubes1989erratum','Taubes, Clifford H.','A note and erratum concerning min-max theory for the {Yang--Mills--Higgs} equations',1989,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01256496',journal='Communications in Mathematical Physics',volume=122,pages='609--613',doi='10.1007/BF01256496',annote='Publisher abstract and metadata opened. Received 21 December 1988. Abstract acknowledges an error and supplies a revised proof of infinitely many nonminimal solutions; full proof not read.')
ref('donaldson1983selfdual','Donaldson, Simon K.','Self-dual connections and the topology of smooth 4-manifolds',1983,'https://scispace.com/pdf/self-dual-connections-and-the-topology-of-smooth-4-manifolds-27wazq56pq.pdf',journal='Bulletin of the American Mathematical Society',volume=8,number=1,pages='81--83',doi='10.1090/S0273-0979-1983-15090-5',annote='Full original three-page announcement read through a scan mirror, including theorem, Lemmas 1--2 and cone singularities. Received 9 August 1982; published January 1983. Receipt is not asserted to be a public preprint date.')
ref('thom1954globales',"Thom, Ren\\'e","Quelques propri\\'et\\'es globales des vari\\'et\\'es diff\\'erentiables",1954,'https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/otherpapers/Thom1954.pdf',journal='Commentarii Mathematici Helvetici',volume=28,pages='17--86',doi='10.1007/BF02566923',annote='Full original opened, Chapter IV, Theorem IV.13 and Corollary IV.14 on pages 81--82 read. The four-dimensional oriented cobordism group and Pontryagin/signature relation are explicit.')
ref('witten1982supersymmetry','Witten, Edward','Supersymmetry and {Morse} theory',1982,'https://math.berkeley.edu/~gbeiner/texts/Witten_Morse_Theory.pdf',journal='Journal of Differential Geometry',volume=17,number=4,pages='661--692',doi='10.4310/jdg/1214437492',annote='Full original opened and Sections 2,4,5 read. Received 26 March 1982; Princeton institutional bibliography confirms December 1982 publication. Exact first public circulation date not located. Original semiclassical argument is distinguished from a complete modern analytic proof.')
ref('enss1983threebody','Enss, Volker','Completeness of three body quantum scattering',1983,'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/BFb0072111',type='incollection',booktitle='Dynamics and Processes',series='Lecture Notes in Mathematics',volume=1031,pages='62--88',publisher='Springer',doi='10.1007/BFb0072111',annote='Publisher abstract and metadata opened; Bielefeld conference 30 November--4 December 1981, publication 1983. Abstract explicitly assumes short-range pair potentials; this is not the Coulomb theorem. Full chapter not read.')
ref('enss1989longrange','Enss, Volker','Long-range scattering of two- and three-body quantum systems',1989,'https://www.numdam.org/item/JEDP_1989____A0_0.pdf',journal="Journ\\'ees \\'Equations aux d\\'eriv\\'ees partielles",pages='1--31',doi='10.5802/jedp.361',annote='Full original read, especially Section 1, equations 1.2--1.13 and Section 4. Coulomb explicitly included; Dollard channel modifiers and removal of implicit subsystem bound-state conditions retained.')
talk(571,'1982-10-11','Jürgen Fröhlich','P001050-P001056','Nonminimal monopoles, four-manifold topology, and Witten’s Morse deformation',r'The entry begins midway down P001051 after Ginzburg and ends on P001054. Fröhlich reports Taubes, Donaldson and Witten, with a final announcement about Enss. The notes distinguish small from zero eigenvalues, but their deformed differential changes sign between pages, their oscillator loses diagonal terms, and its ground state loses the Gaussian. The reconstructions keep the monopole sector, regularity of instanton moduli and long-range scattering modifiers explicit.')
add(571,'The massless Yang–Mills–Higgs energy and magnetic charge','P001051','definition',r'For \(SU(2)\) with a three-component adjoint scalar, \(E=\int(|D_A\varphi|^2+F^2)\,dx\) on \(\mathbb R^3\). A monopole has \(\deg(\varphi|_{S^2_\infty})=1\); minimum-energy solutions are known.',r'Choose a positive invariant inner product on \(\mathfrak{su}_2\), coupling one, finite energy and \(|\Phi|\to v>0\). The normalized asymptotic Higgs field defines \(k=\deg(\Phi/|\Phi|:S^2_\infty\to S^2)\). For \(E=\frac12\int(|B|^2+|D_A\Phi|^2)\), square completion gives \(E\ge4\pi v|k|\), attained by \(B=\pm D_A\Phi\). The absent Higgs potential identifies the Prasad--Sommerfield limit.',r'This is the older Bogomolny framework underlying \citet[abstract and setup]{taubes1982nonminimal1}. The square completion fixes the normalization; no claim that every minimizer is explicitly produced by twistors is needed.',('taubes1982nonminimal1',),reconstruction='Asymptotic norm, positive inner product, factor one-half and magnetic field notation are added.')
add(571,'Nonminimal stationary points and the monopole–antimonopole minimax','P001051','question',r'After the charge-one definition the notes ask for other stationary points, answer yes, cite Taubes, draw a noncontractible loop and compare the solution with a monopole--antimonopole pair. The displayed maximum and minimum are reversed.',r'Does the finite-energy \(SU(2)\) Yang--Mills--Higgs functional in the massless-Higgs limit have critical points outside both Bogomolny equations? The loop method seeks \(c=\inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\sup_{t\in S^1}E(\gamma(t))\), with \(\Gamma\) a fixed nontrivial loop class in the gauge quotient and compactness proved separately. A monopole--antimonopole pair has total charge zero; it does not itself answer a fixed charge-one question.',r'\citet[abstract]{taubes1982nonminimal2} published existence in September 1982, before this talk. The later each-sector multiplicity theorem \citep{taubes1985minmax} requires the corrected argument in \citet{taubes1989erratum}. These papers were checked at publisher-abstract and metadata level.',('taubes1982nonminimal1','taubes1982nonminimal2','taubes1985minmax','taubes1989erratum'),literal='false as written',problem='GP-1982-05',reconstruction='The definite existence question is separated from the ambiguous fixed-sector reading; the variational order is emended.',status='nonminimal finite-energy solutions are established in this massless-Higgs model; sector details are not inferred from the pair picture.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-05',title='Nonminimal finite-energy SU(2) Yang–Mills–Higgs critical points',statement=r'In the Prasad--Sommerfield limit on \(\mathbb R^3\), with \(|\Phi|\to v>0\), do finite-energy stationary points exist outside the Bogomolny minimum equations? A fixed magnetic-charge sector must be specified separately.',occurrences=['1982-571.2'],status=r'As of November 2026: existence was published in September 1982. Later sectorwise multiplicity is covered by Taubes with his 1989 correction. This Higgs-field problem on \(\mathbb R^3\) differs from the earlier pure four-dimensional Yang--Mills problem GP-1977-04.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['taubes1982nonminimal2','taubes1985minmax','taubes1989erratum']))
add(571,'A unit instanton on a simply connected homology four-sphere','P001052','question',r'For compact \(M^4\) with \(\pi_1(M)=1\), \(H^2(M)=0\), and a conformal structure, is there an \(SU(2)\) connection with \(F=\pm *F\) and characteristic class one? The answer is yes, citing Taubes, CMP 1981.',r'For a smooth closed oriented simply connected four-manifold with \(H^2(M;\mathbb R)=0\) and a smooth metric, the charge-one \(SU(2)\) bundle admits an anti-self-dual connection. Equivalently reverse orientation and use self-duality with \(c_2[M]=-1\). The Hodge star on two-forms is conformally invariant.',r'Taubes existence is invoked explicitly in \citet[Section~3, reference~6]{donaldson1983selfdual}. The identifiable original is JDG 17 (1982), 139--170, rather than CMP 1981. Verification is indirect through Donaldson; the original full proof was inaccessible.',('donaldson1983selfdual',),verification='indirect',problem='GP-1982-06',match='exact',reconstruction='Closedness, smoothness, orientation, coefficients and the charge/self-duality sign are specified.',status='existence is established; an instanton does not identify the manifold diffeomorphically with the standard sphere.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-06',title='Existence of a unit instanton when the second cohomology vanishes',statement=r'For every smooth metric on a closed oriented simply connected four-manifold with \(H^2(M;\mathbb R)=0\), does the \(SU(2)\) bundle of charge one admit an anti-self-dual connection?',occurrences=['1982-571.3'],status=r'As of November 2026: yes, by the Taubes result reported in 1982. Verification is indirect through Donaldson, Section 3, citing the exact Taubes theorem; the incorrect notebook journal/year is separately logged.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['donaldson1983selfdual']))
add(571,'The five-dimensional instanton moduli calculation','P001052','claim',r'The parameter space is a five-dimensional manifold, as for the sphere.',r'The charge-one anti-self-dual complex has virtual real dimension \(8-3(1-b_1+b_2^+)=5\). The moduli space is smooth of this dimension near irreducible unobstructed connections. A suitable generic perturbation gives regularity; the index alone does not give a smooth moduli space for every unperturbed metric.',r'\citet[Lemma~1]{donaldson1983selfdual} supplies generic perturbation, using the earlier Atiyah--Hitchin--Singer dimension formula.',('donaldson1983selfdual',),cl='B',verification='read the result',reconstruction='Virtual dimension, actual regularity and stabilizers are distinguished.')
add(571,'The instanton end and a five-dimensional cobordism','P001052','construction',r'The parameter space \(B\) has boundary diffeomorphic to \(M^4\).',r'The charge-one moduli space has a bubbling end parametrized by concentration point and scale, \(M\times(0,\varepsilon)\). After regular perturbation and compactification, absence of reducibles in the \(H^2=0\) case yields a compact oriented five-manifold with boundary \(M\). The boundary is not already contained in the smooth space of connections.',r'\citet[Lemma~2]{donaldson1983selfdual} gives the product end and compact complement. Received 9 August 1982, published January 1983; an exact earlier public circulation date was not found.',('donaldson1983selfdual',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Bubbling compactification and perturbation are restored.')
add(571,'Oriented four-dimensional cobordism and the signature','P001052','theorem',r'\(M^4\) is a boundary iff its Pontryagin class vanishes, equivalently its intersection-form signature vanishes.',r'For smooth closed oriented \(M^4\), oriented null-cobordism is equivalent to \(\sigma(M)=0\), or \(\langle p_1(TM),[M]\rangle=0\). Indeed \(\Omega_4^{SO}\simeq\mathbb Z\), generated by \(\mathbb{CP}^2\), and \(\langle p_1,[M]\rangle=3\sigma(M)\). For connected oriented \(M\), the number also detects the top-degree integral class.',r'This is old material: \citet[Theorem~IV.13 and Corollary~IV.14]{thom1954globales}.',('thom1954globales',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Oriented cobordism and the characteristic number specify the shorthand.')
add(571,'Donaldson’s diagonalization theorem versus a manifold classification','P001052','theorem',r'A positive-definite form implies that the manifold is an \ru{обобщ. проект. плоск. \(CP^2\)}; hence its integral form is a sum of squares.',r'If \(M\) is smooth, closed, oriented and simply connected with positive-definite intersection form, then \(Q_M\simeq_{\mathbb Z}\operatorname{diag}(1,\ldots,1)\). The theorem does not assert \(M\cong\#_{b_2}\mathbb{CP}^2\) by diffeomorphism.',r'The exact conclusion is \citet[Theorem, p.~81]{donaldson1983selfdual}. Fröhlich reports Donaldson; the manifold-equality wording exceeds that theorem.',('donaldson1983selfdual',),cl='B',literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The conclusion is restricted to integral forms.')
add(571,'Reducible instantons and cones on the complex projective plane','P001052','construction',r'For the positive-definite case, \(B\) has singularities that are cones over \(\mathbb{CP}^2\).',r'In this charge-one self-dual problem, suitable perturbations leave isolated reducibles modeled on \(\mathbb C^3/S^1=C(\mathbb{CP}^2)\). Their number is \(n(Q)=\frac12\#\{a:Q(a)=1\}\). Removing cone neighborhoods gives a cobordism between \(M\) and \(n(Q)\) projective planes; signature and the norm-one-vector bound force diagonalization.',r'The local model and count appear in \citet[Sections~3--4]{donaldson1983selfdual}.',('donaldson1983selfdual',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Charge, regularity, reducibility and the lattice count are supplied.')
add(571,'The weak Morse inequalities and their hypotheses','P001052','theorem',r'For isolated critical points, \(M_p\) counts points with \(p\) negative Hessian eigenvalues; \(M_p\ge B_p\).',r'For a smooth Morse function on a smooth closed manifold, let \(m_p\) count its nondegenerate critical points of index \(p\). Then \(m_p\ge b_p(M;\mathbb R)\). Isolated degenerate critical points cannot in general be counted solely by their negative Hessian eigenvalues.',r'These classical inequalities are the starting point of \citet[Section~2]{witten1982supersymmetry}.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='Closedness and nondegeneracy are restored.')
add(571,'Conjugating the de Rham differential','P001052-P001053','construction',r'\(d_t=e^{-tf}de^{tf}\), \(d_t^2=0\), and unchanged cohomology are followed by \(d_t=d-t\,df\wedge\).',r'The conjugation gives \(d_t=d+t\,df\wedge\), \(d_t^*=d^*+t\,\iota_{\nabla f}\). Multiplication by \(e^{tf}\) is a chain isomorphism \((\Omega^*,d_t)\to(\Omega^*,d)\), proving nilpotence and unchanged cohomology. The two later minuses instead correspond to \(t\mapsto-t\).',r'See \citet[Section~2, equations~(9)--(10)]{witten1982supersymmetry}. Received 26 March 1982, published December 1982; receipt is not a verified public-preprint date.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The consistent sign and chain isomorphism replace the loose appeal to homotopy.')
add(571,'Deformed Hodge representatives','P001052-P001053','construction',r'Representatives satisfy \(d_t\omega=0\) and \((\omega,d_t\beta)=0\), hence \(\Box_t\omega=0\).',r'On a closed Riemannian manifold, \(\Delta_t=d_t^*d_t+d_td_t^*\) is nonnegative self-adjoint elliptic, with \(\langle\Delta_t\omega,\omega\rangle=\|d_t\omega\|^2+\|d_t^*\omega\|^2\). Deformed harmonic forms represent its cohomology, so \(\dim\ker(\Delta_t|_{\Omega^p})=b_p\) for every finite \(t\).',r'This is the Hodge step in \citet[Section~2, equation~(11)]{witten1982supersymmetry}.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Compactness and the analytic operator setting are explicit.')
add(571,'Exterior algebra as a fermionic Fock space','P001053-P001054','construction',r'Kirillov identifies \(a_i\) with contraction and \(a_i^*\) with exterior multiplication, correcting the initial description of \(a_i\). The mixed anticommutator is \(\delta_{ij}\), and \(a_i\Omega_0=0\).',r'For an orthonormal one-particle basis, \(\mathcal F_-=\Lambda^*\mathbb C^n\), \(a_i^*=e_i\wedge\), \(a_i=\iota_{e_i}\), \(\Omega_0=1\). Besides \(\{a_i,a_j^*\}=\delta_{ij}\), one has \(\{a_i,a_j\}=\{a_i^*,a_j^*\}=0\). Ordered creation products form the exterior basis; an unrestricted antisymmetric coefficient sum requires a matching factorial convention.',r'The standard fermionic algebra is recalled in \citet[Section~2, before equation~(13)]{witten1982supersymmetry}.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The omitted anticommutators and coefficient convention are supplied.',speaker='A. A. Kirillov and Jürgen Fröhlich')
add(571,'The Witten Laplacian and its Hessian term','P001054','construction',r'\(\Box_t=\Box+t^2(df)^2+t\sum[a_i^*,a_j]\partial_i\partial_jf\).',r'For the plus-sign differential, in an orthonormal frame, \[\Delta_t=\Delta+t^2|df|^2+t\sum_{i,j}(\nabla^2f)_{ij}[a_i^*,a_j].\] The Hessian is covariant. On functions its last term is \(-t\operatorname{tr}(\nabla^2f)\), independently checked by applying \(d_t^*d_t\).',r'Compare \citet[Section~2, equation~(13)]{witten1982supersymmetry}. The zero-form check is supplied here \ours.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Covariant derivatives and the nonnegative Laplacian convention are restored.')
add(571,'The normal harmonic oscillator at a Morse point','P001054','construction',r'For \(f=f_0+\sum s_i\lambda_i y_i^2+O(|y|^3)\), the potential is \(4t^2\sum\lambda_i^2y_i^2\), but the Hessian sum uses \(i<j\) and marginal factors four.',r'For \(\lambda_i>0\), \(s_i=\pm1\), the quadratic model is \[H_t^{\rm quad}=\sum_i(-\partial_{y_i}^2+4t^2\lambda_i^2y_i^2)+2t\sum_i s_i\lambda_i[a_i^*,a_i].\] The Hessian has diagonal entries \(2s_i\lambda_i\); restricting to \(i<j\) loses them all. Since \([a_i^*,a_i]=2N_i-1\), the bosonic ground energy cancels for occupation \(N_i=0\) in positive directions and \(N_i=1\) in negative directions.',r'See \citet[Section~2, equations~(15)--(18)]{witten1982supersymmetry}. The notebook normalization gives the factor two, independently checked \ours.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Diagonal terms and their coefficient are emended.')
add(571,'The missing Gaussian in the local ground state','P001054','claim',r'The operator allegedly annihilates \(dy_{n-p+1}\wedge\cdots\wedge dy_n\).',r'The unique square-integrable zero mode of the quadratic model, up to scalar, is \[\omega_{0,t}=e^{-t\sum_i\lambda_i y_i^2}dy_{n-p+1}\wedge\cdots\wedge dy_n.\] The bare wedge is neither annihilated nor square-integrable on \(\mathbb R^n\). Direct differentiation verifies \(H_t^{\rm quad}\omega_{0,t}=0\); all other oscillator/Fock occupations have positive energy.',r'The degree selection matches \citet[Section~2, equation~(18)]{witten1982supersymmetry}. The Gaussian substitution is checked here \ours.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The scalar Gaussian is an essential emendation.')
add(571,'Small eigenvalues, quasimodes and the Morse inequalities','P001054','method',r'The notes explicitly seek small rather than necessarily zero eigenvalues; each critical point gives an approximate eigenfunction.',r'On a closed manifold with Morse \(f\), for large \(t\) the low-energy spectral subspace of \(\Delta_t\) in degree \(p\) has dimension \(m_p\), containing the \(b_p\)-dimensional kernel. Cut-off local Gaussians are quasimodes; their small eigenvalues need not vanish. The low-energy subcomplex yields strong and weak Morse inequalities.',r'\citet[Section~2]{witten1982supersymmetry} presents the semiclassical mechanism. Its original asymptotic argument is distinguished from a complete modern analytic proof.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='A spectral cutoff, the large-parameter regime and the subcomplex make the shorthand precise.')
add(571,'Gelfand’s replacement of cycle modifications by form modifications','P001054','remark',r'Gelfand says \ru{вместо перестроек в циклах --- перестройки в формах}, connecting differential operators with Morse modifications.',r'The remark describes the passage from handle attachments and cycles to spectral localization of the de Rham complex near critical points. It proposes no theorem beyond the surrounding construction.',r'This is a conceptual reading of \citet[Section~2]{witten1982supersymmetry}.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',literal='correct',verification='read the result',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
add(571,'Supersymmetry as geometry of field configurations','P001054','method',r'\(d_t\) is a supercharge; an expression with \(\delta/\delta\varphi(x)\) and \(ta^*(x)\varphi^n(x)\) is compared with \(d+t\,df\wedge\). In two dimensions fermions are forms on bosonic configuration space.',r'Finite-dimensional supersymmetric quantum mechanics uses \(d_t,d_t^*\). For a theory on a spatial circle, the analogous configuration space is a space of maps. The form picture is formal until domains, a measure and regularized operators are specified. The functional derivative displayed in the notes alone does not define a quantum field operator.',r'\citet[Sections~4--5]{witten1982supersymmetry} develops the loop-space analogy and explicitly leaves analytic foundations incomplete.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='B',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The finite-dimensional result is separated from the formal infinite-dimensional analogy.')
add(571,'The proposed higher-dimensional gauge-field interpretation','P001054','question',r'For \(d>2\), gauge fields may be needed for an analogous interpretation, which is unavailable. A parenthesis calls the Poincaré group \ru{простая}.',r'No supersymmetry algebra, target, signature, gauge group or proposed complex is specified, so no single unresolved problem is determined. If \ru{простая} means algebraically simple, it is false because translations form a proper normal subgroup; in context it may mean uncomplicated.',r'\citet[Section~5]{witten1982supersymmetry} discusses Kähler scalar theories and an unclear gauge-theory structure. This thematic match does not identify the missing target.',('witten1982supersymmetry',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',verification='read the result',match='thematic only',confidence='medium',status='the notebook does not specify a definite higher-dimensional problem whose present openness can be assessed.')
add(571,'Enss’s announcement of three-body Coulomb asymptotic completeness','P001054','theorem',r'Enss is credited with asymptotic completeness for three particles with Coulomb interaction.',r'For three nonrelativistic particles in \(\mathbb R^3\) with pair Coulomb potentials, after removing center-of-mass motion, the modified channel wave operators exist and their orthogonal ranges exhaust the continuous spectral subspace. Channels include three separated particles and each bound pair plus a third particle. Long-range Dollard modifiers account for Coulomb phases.',r'The 1983 chapter \citet[abstract]{enss1983threebody}, from a 1981 conference, is explicitly short-range. The Coulomb scope is verified in \citet[Section~1, equations~(1.12)--(1.13), Section~4]{enss1989longrange}, which removes earlier implicit subsystem conditions. The precise 1982 announced version was not located.',('enss1983threebody','enss1989longrange'),cl='B',verification='read the result',problem='GP-1982-07',reconstruction='Quantum mechanics, channel projections and long-range comparison dynamics are supplied.',status='three-body Coulomb completeness is established; the search does not certify that every detail of the 1989 version was already proved in October 1982.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-07',title='Asymptotic completeness for three quantum particles with Coulomb forces',statement=r'For the center-of-mass reduced three-body Schrödinger Hamiltonian with pair Coulomb interactions, do Dollard-modified channels exhaust the continuous spectral subspace?',occurrences=['1982-571.20'],status=r'As of November 2026: established in Enss 1989, equations (1.12)--(1.13). The 1983 chapter is short-range and does not verify the Coulomb claim. Earlier 1971-252.3 is a thematic wave-operator discussion without an identical problem ID.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['enss1983threebody','enss1989longrange']))
corr('P001051','Minimax and sector transition',r'\(\max_t\min_{\rm curve}\); a monopole--antimonopole pair after a charge-one definition.',r'\(\inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\sup_{t\in S^1}E(\gamma(t))\). The pair has total charge zero.',r'Scan P001051 confirms the order and pair. The initial subscript is doubtful. The variational order is an emendation; degree addition fixes total charge.')
corr('P001052','Taubes unit-instanton reference','Taubes, Comm. Math. Phys., 1981.','Taubes, Self-dual Yang--Mills connections on non-self-dual four-manifolds, JDG 17 (1982), 139--170.','Scan P001052 confirms journal/year. Donaldson reference 6 and Taubes Part I reference 24 identify the JDG work; full original proof not read.',type='provenance')
corr('P001052','Moduli dimension and boundary',r'The moduli space is a five-manifold with boundary \(M\).','The dimension is virtual; regularity needs unobstructedness or perturbation. The boundary comes from bubbling compactification.','Donaldson Lemmas 1--2 state generic perturbation and a product end with compact complement. Scan P001052 inspected.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001052','Pontryagin class and bounding',r'Boundary iff the Pontryagin class vanishes.',r'For closed oriented \(M\), oriented null-cobordism iff \(\langle p_1(TM),[M]\rangle=3\sigma(M)=0\).','Thom IV.13--IV.14 checked. Orientation and characteristic-number interpretation matter; connected oriented top-degree classes are detected by the number.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001052','Donaldson manifold equality',r'The manifold equals a generalized \(\mathbb{CP}^2\).',r'Its integral form is \(\operatorname{diag}(1,\ldots,1)\); no diffeomorphism classification follows.','Scan P001052 confirms the wording with nearby cancellation. Donaldson page 81 states diagonalization.')
corr('P001052-P001053','Conjugation and equation (2)',r'\(d_t=e^{-tf}de^{tf}\), then \(d_t=d-t\,df\wedge\), \(d_t^*=d^*-t\,\iota_{\nabla f}\).','Both later signs are plus for the given conjugation.',r'Both scanned minuses are clear. Leibniz gives \(e^{-tf}d(e^{tf}\omega)=d\omega+t\,df\wedge\omega\).')
corr('P001053','Creation and annihilation prose',r'The unstarred \(a_i\) is exterior multiplication.',r'\(a_i=\iota_{e_i}\) contracts; \(a_i^*=e_i^\flat\wedge\) creates.','Kirillov explicitly corrects the earlier phrase in scan P001053; the vacuum convention agrees.')
corr('P001054','Equation (9) and marginal coefficient',r'\(t\sum_{i<j}[a_i^*,a_j]c_{ij}\), with marginal coefficient \(4t\).',r'For \(f=\sum_i s_i\lambda_i y_i^2\), use \(2t\sum_i s_i\lambda_i[a_i^*,a_i]\).',r'Scan P001054 confirms \(i<j\) and four. The Hessian entries are \(2s_i\lambda_i\), independently agreeing with oscillator cancellation.')
corr('P001054','Equation (10), annihilated form',r'\(dy_{n-p+1}\wedge\cdots\wedge dy_n\) alone.',r'\(e^{-t\sum_i\lambda_i y_i^2}dy_{n-p+1}\wedge\cdots\wedge dy_n\).','The scan has only the wedge. Substitution and square-integrability require the Gaussian.')
corr('P001054','Poincaré group parenthesis',r'The group is \ru{простая}, translated simple.','If uncomplicated is intended, say so; the group is not algebraically simple.','Translations form a proper normal subgroup. The colloquial Russian meaning remains possible; scan inspected.',type='ambiguity')
audit.append('## Record 571\nActual boundary: midway P001051 through P001054; Russian fully read and all four scans inspected, including minimax loop, charge transition, instanton citation, differential signs and oscillator annotations. Taubes Part I DOI10.1007/BF01206014 and Part II DOI10.1007/BF01212170 opened at publisher abstract/metadata level, June/September1982; 1985 DOI10.1007/BF01221215 and necessary1989erratum DOI10.1007/BF01256496 similarly. Donaldson full original https://scispace.com/pdf/self-dual-connections-and-the-topology-of-smooth-4-manifolds-27wazq56pq.pdf read, theorem/Lemmas1--2/cone argument; Taubes existence verified indirectly there. Thom full https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/otherpapers/Thom1954.pdf IV.13--IV.14 read. Witten full https://math.berkeley.edu/~gbeiner/texts/Witten_Morse_Theory.pdf Sections2,4,5 read; December1982 publication distinct from March receipt. Enss1983 DOI10.1007/BFb0072111 abstract short-range; Enss1989 full https://www.numdam.org/item/JEDP_1989____A0_0.pdf Section1 andSection4, Dollard channels and Coulomb inclusion read. No exact1982 announced version located. Previous index: GP-1977-04 is pure four-dimensional Yang--Mills, not R3adjoint-Higgs; 1971-252.3 genericwaveoperators only thematic. Direct checks: degree addition, index5, conjugation, CAR, diagonal Hessian, Gaussian cancellation and Poincare translations. No additional C/D from unspecified field-space remarks.')
